    description: "FOUND 2026-10-04 by WI-tumol (branch jgstern-agent/fix/tumol). The receive-half sibling of WI-tumol, through a different object.\n\n`f = sock.makefile(); line = f.readline()` reads the far side's bytes, and nothing mints untrusted_input there. library_signatures/python.yaml declares `socket.socket.makefile: io.BufferedRWPair`, which is the right type only for mode \"rwb\"/\"rw\" with buffering; the default mode \"r\" returns an io.TextIOWrapper and \"rb\" an io.BufferedReader (CPython Lib/socket.py makefile). No io_primitives row exists under any of those types for a socket-backed file, and io.TextIOWrapper/BufferedReader are also the types of ordinary files.\n\nUntil WI-tumol the row was unconsumed (member rows did not type a typed receiver's result). WI-tumol's member-return propagation makes it live: on a typed socket, `f.readline()` now arrives at `python:io.BufferedRWPair:0-0:readline` (unclassified) instead of `python:external:0-0:readline` (untyped receiver). Verdicts on the fixture did not move (the socket.socket.makefile call itself is already unclassified, so the file was already inconclusive), but the row now types a wrong class.\n\nFIX SHAPE (to verify, not asserted): decide what a socket-backed file is (a synthetic module slot like the `file` one py.py uses for open(), stamped from makefile's mode argument?), row its reads net_recv and its writes net_send, and correct or remove the BufferedRWPair row. Needs its own A/B."  # 7765
